# Session-Based Implementation: Processor Replacement Patterns

## Purpose
Guide the replacement of mock implementations with real business logic within individual sessions while maintaining test compatibility and code quality standards.

## Session Pre-Implementation Checklist

Before writing any implementation code in current session:
- [ ] Session plan reviewed (plan/sessions/session-N-phase-M.md)
- [ ] E2E test is written and failing
- [ ] Prisma migration created and applied
- [ ] Zod domain schemas defined (layered architecture)
- [ ] Repository interface designed
- [ ] Service layer structure planned
- [ ] Previous session dependencies confirmed

## Code Style Guidelines

Full style rules live in `rules/node-standards.mdc`; this section covers implementation-specific additions.

### Explicit Return Types
Always annotate exported function/method return types:
```typescript
// Good
async function createPost(data: PostCreate): Promise<Post> { ... }

// Bad
async function createPost(data: PostCreate) { ... }
```

### TSDoc Comments
Use TSDoc for all public services/repositories:
```typescript
/**
 * Create a new blog post.
 *
 * @param data - Post creation data containing title, content, and author ID.
 * @returns The created post with generated ID and timestamps.
 * @throws {Error} If the database operation fails.
 */
async function createPost(data: PostCreate): Promise<Post> { ... }
```

### Function Length
Keep functions focused and short:
- Prefer under ~50 lines per function
- Single responsibility principle
- Extract complex logic into helper functions

### No `any`
Use `unknown` and narrow, or a precise interface/Zod-inferred type. `any` disables the type checker entirely and is banned by ESLint (`@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any`).

## Session Processor Replacement Pattern

### Step 1: Review Session Plan
Review plan/sessions/session-N-phase-M.md to understand:
- Current session objectives
- Endpoint to be implemented
- Dependencies from previous sessions
- Expected deliverables

### Step 2: Identify Mock Code
Locate the mock implementation from Session 1 (Phase 0):
```typescript
// src/controllers/posts.controller.ts
export async function createPost(req: Request, res: Response): Promise<void> {
  // MOCK IMPLEMENTATION
  res.status(201).json({
    postId: 'mock-123',
    title: req.body.title,
    content: req.body.content,
    authorId: 'mock-author',
    createdAt: '2024-01-01T00:00:00Z',
  });
}
```

### Step 3: Add Service Layer
Import and initialize service (via a small factory or manual DI in the controller module):
```typescript
import { PostService } from '../services/post.service';
import { PostRepository } from '../repositories/post.repository';
import { prisma } from '../config/prisma';

const postService = new PostService(new PostRepository(prisma));
```

### Step 4: Validate the Request
Parse and validate with Zod before touching the service:
```typescript
export async function createPost(req: Request, res: Response): Promise<void> {
  const parsed = CreatePostRequestSchema.parse(req.body);
```

### Step 5: Call Service Layer
Execute business logic through the service, mapping thrown errors to HTTP status codes:
```typescript
  try {
    const created = await postService.createPost({
      title: parsed.title,
      content: parsed.content,
      authorId: req.user!.userId,
    });
    res.status(201).json(created);
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof ValidationError) {
      res.status(400).json({ detail: error.message });
      return;
    }
    logger.error({ err: error }, 'Error creating post');
    res.status(500).json({ detail: 'Internal server error' });
  }
}
```

### Complete Implementation
```typescript
/**
 * Create a new blog post.
 *
 * Request: CreatePostRequest (title, content)
 * Response: PostResponse (postId, title, content, authorId, createdAt)
 * Auth: required (JWT Bearer token, injects req.user)
 */
export async function createPost(req: Request, res: Response): Promise<void> {
  const parsed = CreatePostRequestSchema.parse(req.body);

  try {
    const created = await postService.createPost({
      title: parsed.title,
      content: parsed.content,
      authorId: req.user!.userId,
    });
    res.status(201).json(created);
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof ValidationError) {
      res.status(400).json({ detail: error.message });
      return;
    }
    logger.error({ err: error }, 'Error creating post');
    res.status(500).json({ detail: 'Internal server error' });
  }
}
```

Note Zod's `.parse()` throwing a `ZodError` should be caught centrally by the error-handling middleware (see below) rather than in every controller — prefer wrapping route handlers so `ZodError` maps to 400 automatically.

## Error Handling Patterns

### Input Validation
Zod schemas provide automatic validation and refinement:
```typescript
export const CreatePostRequestSchema = z.object({
  title: z.string().min(1).max(200).transform((v) => v.trim()),
  content: z.string().min(1).max(10000),
  authorId: z.string(),
}).refine((data) => data.title.length > 0, {
  message: 'Title cannot be empty',
  path: ['title'],
});
```

### Business Logic Errors
Handle in the service layer with typed custom errors:
```typescript
export class ValidationError extends Error {}
export class NotFoundError extends Error {}

export class PostService {
  async createPost(data: PostCreate): Promise<Post> {
    if (data.title.length < 5) {
      throw new ValidationError('Title must be at least 5 characters');
    }
    try {
      return await this.postRepo.create(data);
    } catch (error) {
      logger.error({ err: error }, 'Repository error');
      throw error;
    }
  }
}
```

### Centralized Error Middleware
Map domain errors to HTTP status codes in one place instead of per-controller try/catch:
```typescript
// src/middleware/errorHandler.ts
import { ErrorRequestHandler } from 'express';
import { ZodError } from 'zod';
import { ValidationError, NotFoundError } from '../errors';

export const errorHandler: ErrorRequestHandler = (err, _req, res, _next) => {
  if (err instanceof ZodError) {
    res.status(400).json({ detail: err.issues });
    return;
  }
  if (err instanceof ValidationError) {
    res.status(400).json({ detail: err.message });
    return;
  }
  if (err instanceof NotFoundError) {
    res.status(404).json({ detail: err.message });
    return;
  }
  logger.error({ err }, 'Unexpected error');
  res.status(500).json({ detail: 'Internal server error' });
};
```

## Logging Patterns

### Use Structured Logging
```typescript
import { logger } from '../config/logger';

// Good: structured with context
logger.info({ postId: post.id, authorId: post.authorId, titleLength: post.title.length }, 'Post created');

// Bad: unstructured
console.log(`Created post ${post.id}`);
```

### Log Levels
- `debug`: Detailed information for debugging
- `info`: General informational messages
- `warn`: Warning messages for recoverable issues
- `error`: Error messages for failures
- `fatal`: Critical errors requiring immediate attention (pino)

### What to Log
```typescript
// Log significant operations
logger.info({ authorId }, 'Creating post for author');

// Log errors with context
logger.error({ err: error }, 'Failed to create post');

// Log performance issues
logger.warn({ durationMs: duration }, 'Slow query detected');

// Never log secrets
logger.info({ userId: user.id }, 'User logged in'); // Good
logger.info({ password: user.password }, 'User logged in'); // BAD - NEVER LOG SECRETS
```

## Database Transaction Patterns

### Single Operation
```typescript
async createPost(data: PostCreate): Promise<Post> {
  return this.postRepo.create(data);
}
```

### Multiple Operations
```typescript
async createPostWithTags(data: PostCreate, tags: string[]): Promise<Post> {
  return this.prisma.$transaction(async (tx) => {
    const post = await tx.post.create({ data });
    await tx.postTag.createMany({
      data: tags.map((tag) => ({ postId: post.id, tag })),
    });
    return post;
  });
}
```

## Performance Considerations

### Avoid N+1 Queries
```typescript
// Bad: N+1 query problem
const posts = await this.postRepo.getAll();
for (const post of posts) {
  post.author = await this.userRepo.getById(post.authorId);
}

// Good: single query with Prisma `include`
const postsWithAuthors = await this.prisma.post.findMany({ include: { author: true } });
```

### Use Pagination
```typescript
async getPosts(limit = 20, offset = 0): Promise<Post[]> {
  return this.postRepo.getAll(limit, offset); // maps to Prisma `take`/`skip`
}
```

### Cache Expensive Operations
```typescript
import LRUCache from 'lru-cache';

const statsCache = new LRUCache<string, PostStatistics>({ max: 100, ttl: 60_000 });

function getPostStatistics(postId: string): PostStatistics {
  const cached = statsCache.get(postId);
  if (cached) return cached;
  const stats = calculateStatistics(postId);
  statsCache.set(postId, stats);
  return stats;
}
```

For distributed caching across multiple app instances, use Redis instead of an in-process LRU cache.

## Session Testing Implementation

After replacing the processor in the current session:

### Run E2E Tests
```bash
npx jest tests/routes/posts.test.ts -t "creates a post"
```

### Verify Test Passes
Tests should now pass with the real implementation:
- Actual database IDs generated
- Real timestamps created
- Data persisted correctly

### Add Edge Case Tests
```typescript
it('rejects an overly long title', async () => { /* ... */ });
it('prevents duplicate resource creation', async () => { /* ... */ });
```

## Session Code Review Checklist

Before marking session implementation complete:
- [ ] Session plan objectives met
- [ ] Explicit return types on all exported functions
- [ ] TSDoc comments on all public services/repositories
- [ ] Error handling implemented
- [ ] Logging added for operations
- [ ] No hardcoded values
- [ ] No secrets in code
- [ ] Proper Prisma transaction handling where needed
- [ ] Performance considerations addressed
- [ ] E2E tests passing for current endpoint
- [ ] Code formatted with Prettier
- [ ] No TODO comments without tickets
- [ ] Session summary documents implementation

## Session Implementation Anti-Patterns

Do NOT:
- Skip session plan review
- Put business logic in controllers
- Access the repository directly from a route/controller
- Return raw Prisma model objects to the API (always map through the repository/service)
- Use bare `catch {}` blocks that swallow errors
- Log sensitive information
- Hardcode configuration values
- Skip error handling
- Use `any` instead of a precise type
- Use module-level mutable global state
- Implement features not required by the current session
- Implement endpoints from other sessions
- Skip session documentation
